## 3. Threshold Color Schemes & Tiers

The dashboard uses threshold colors to make abnormal operating conditions visually identifiable.

| Panel Name | Metric Unit | Nominal (Green) | Warning (Yellow/Orange) | Critical (Red) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| P95 API Latency | ms | Below 500 ms | 500 ms to below 1000 ms | 1000 ms and above |
| CPU Utilization | % | Normal CPU utilization range | Elevated CPU utilization | High/critical CPU utilization |
| HTTP Error Rate | % | Below 1% | 1% to below 5% | 5% and above |

### P95 API Latency

The JSON configuration defines:

- **Green:** Base/normal condition
- **Yellow:** Starts at 500 ms
- **Red:** Starts at 1000 ms

Therefore:

- Below 500 ms = Nominal
- 500–999 ms = Warning
- 1000 ms and above = Critical

This threshold scheme highlights services approaching or exceeding one second of P95 request latency.

### CPU Utilization

The CPU panel is configured as a percentage gauge with:

- **Minimum:** 0%
- **Maximum:** 100%

The gauge provides a direct view of CPU utilization by instance. Increasing CPU utilization represents decreasing available CPU headroom. Threshold colors should therefore progress from normal to warning and critical as CPU utilization approaches the upper end of the 0–100% range.

### HTTP Error Rate

The JSON configuration defines:

- **Green:** Base/normal condition
- **Yellow:** Starts at 1.0%
- **Red:** Starts at 5.0%

Therefore:

- Below 1% = Nominal
- 1% to below 5% = Warning
- 5% and above = Critical

The HTTP error-rate panel therefore makes persistent server-side failures immediately visible.

